ovides an optimization-flavored instance with an analytic optimum check.\n C. (Supplement to b3) small optimization-based meta-learning step (k unrolled gradient steps).\n\n============================================================\n2) CONTROLS AND PROTOCOL\n============================================================\nCONTROL (i) \u2014 PRIMARY, PyTorch default autograd:\n Run the EXACT same forward solver loop inside the autograd scope (torch.enable_grad), then torch.autograd.backward through all T iterations (memory ~ O(T)). This is the \"standard autograd implementation\" the question refers to.\n\nCONTROL (ii) \u2014 OPTIONAL cross-framework (documented as an assumption, runs only if JAX is available in the execution environment):\n Same unrolled solve expressed with jax.numpy, gradients via jax.grad (reverse, vjp under the hood) and, where relevant, jax.jacfwd for forward-mode; comparison is cross-framework and reported separately (not in the primary decision) because forward-loop representation may differ slightly.\n\nCONTROL (iii) \u2014 MANUAL IMPLICIT-DIFF (torch.autograd.Function), IDEAL:\n A custom torch.autograd.Function whose forward stores y* and whose backward recomputes the needed Jacobian-vector products on the fly (via a few extra function evaluations / autograd.grad calls) WITHOUT a persistent tape and WITHOUT caching the linear-solve results. This isolates the \"cache/reuse\" factor from the \"implicit-vs-explicit\" factor:\n - treatment vs (i): isolates (cache + explicit) vs tape-unrolling.\n - treatment vs (iii): isolates the caching/reuse benefit with implicit math held out.\n - (iii) vs (i): isolates the tape-O(T)-memory factor.\n\nPROTOCOL (identical across treatment and controls):\n - Shared generator: deterministic seeded problem instances; same theta, x, solver, iteration count T, stopping rule \u2014 iterate sequences are bit-identical across pipelines whenever the differentiation method cannot alter the forward arithmetic (verify by 