he linear-solve results. This isolates the \"cache/reuse\" factor from the \"implicit-vs-explicit\" factor:\n - treatment vs (i): isolates (cache + explicit) vs tape-unrolling.\n - treatment vs (iii): isolates the caching/reuse benefit with implicit math held out.\n - (iii) vs (i): isolates the tape-O(T)-memory factor.\n\nPROTOCOL (identical across treatment and controls):\n - Shared generator: deterministic seeded problem instances; same theta, x, solver, iteration count T, stopping rule \u2014 iterate sequences are bit-identical across pipelines whenever the differentiation method cannot alter the forward arithmetic (verify by checking max |y*_A - y*_B| ~ machine epsilon prior to timing; if the explicit path perturbs the forward, record and report this divergence).\n - For each (task, parameter setting): >=5 paired repeats (same seed across repeats), warm-up runs discarded, report mean +/- std.\n - Order of pipelines randomized round-robin to reduce drift; each pipeline timed in its own subprocess where feasible to isolate memory.\n - Memory: torch-allocated peak via torch.autograd.profiler.profile(profile_memory=True) (CPU in this env; cuda if available), plus tracemalloc/RSS as fallback; record peak vs T.\n - Timing: wall-clock forward phase and backward phase separately (time.perf_counter).\n\n============================================================\n3) METRICS, DIMENSIONS, ANALYSIS PLAN\n============================================================\nMETRICS (per instance x pipeline):\n - forward time, backward time, total time (mean +/- std, min/median/max across repeats).\n - peak memory and memory growth slope vs T.\n - gradient error: relative error of the computed dL/dtheta vs reference. Reference = central finite differences (1e-6 perturbation, high-precision accumulation) where feasible, or analytic Jacobian for task B.\n - numerical stability: fraction of runs with NaN/Inf in gradient; relative gradient error on well- vs ill-conditioned sets; solution accuracy (|y* - true optimum|).\n\nDIMENSIONS SWEPT:\n - n = dimension of y (e.g., {32, 128, 512, 1024}) -> time & memory scaling.\n - T = forward iterations (e.g., {2, 5, 10, 20}) -> memory vs T curve (where tape O(T) should grow).\n - kappa = condition number of (I - J_f) or of the Hessian/linear system, set by diagonal scaling, kappa in {~1 (well), ~1e3, ~1e6, near-singular ~1e8} -> stability dimension, including the near-singular regime where numerical error can explode.\n - batch size: optional {1, 16} (only if budget allows).\n\nANALYSIS PLAN:\n - Paired comparisons: for each (task, n, T, kappa) cell, paired difference (treatment - control(i)) on per-repeat measurements; report mean diff and std, and a paired test (e.g., Wilcoxon signed-rank or paired t on log-time) at the cell level; since runs are seeded, pairing is exact.\n - Scaling analysis: log-log slope of time vs n and of peak memory vs T for treatment vs control(i) to demonstrate the O(1)-vs-O(T) memory claim.\n - Stability analysis: gradient relative error vs kappa (log-log), NaN frequency per setting; check whether treatment diverges later than control(i) as kappa -> near-singular (i.e., whether it is more stable, worse, or equal).\n - Aggregation for the decision: well-conditioned set = all cells with kappa <= 1e3, excluding near-singular; ill-conditioned set = kappa >= 1e6.\n - Sensitivity: report how the conclusion changes if thresholds are +/-5pp and error tolerance 1e-3/1e-5 (decision-rule sensitivity table).\n\nVIABILITY / FEASIBILITY (honest statement):\n - Not executable in the current workspace (Python 3.14, no deps, no network). Feasible in a clean env with torch (>=2.x, CPU is sufficient) + torchopt matching. Estimated compute: primary grid ~ (2 tasks x 4 n x 4 T x 4 kappa x 2 batch) x 3 pipelines x 5 repeats ~ 4-6k backward calls of small matrices; at CPU (224 cores available in the intended runtime class) this is on the order of ~10-30 min. JAX control adds install/setup risk and is conditional on availability.\n\n============================================================\n4) DECISION RULE (AGREED) AND ASSUMPTIONS\n============================================================\nAGREED DECISION RULE \u2014 the treatment is declared \"better\" ONLY IF, over the well-conditioned set and averaged across the primary grid:\n (r1) backward/total time >= 10% lower than control (i) (geometric mean ratio <= 0.90), AND\n (r2) peak memory >= 10% lower than control (i) (ratio <= 0.90), with a clear flattening of memory vs T (slope ~0 vs ~T), AND\n (r3) mean gradient relative error within 1e-4 of the reference (i.e., both treatment and control accurate; treatment not allowed to buy speed with accuracy), AND\n (r4) NO stability regression on the ill-conditioned set: NaN/Inf frequency not higher than the control's rate (within 2 runs), and mean relative gradient error not larger than the control's by more than 10%.\n If r1-r4 hold -> claim supported; if only r2 holds -> \"memory benefit but no time benefit\"; if only r3 fails -> \"speed at the cost of accuracy, not an improvement\". The decision applies to the b1 primary path; b3 is reported separately.\n\nASSUMPTIONS (each with sensitivity):\n A1) TorchOpt version (target: latest stable, e.g., 0.7.x) and exact symbol/signature in torchopt.diff.explicit. SENSITIVITY: API-pinning step + thin wrapper makes the harness version-robust; if the symbol differs, the wrapper is updated, not the benchmark logic.\n A2) PyTorch is the reference backend (JAX control is optional/cross-framework). SENSITIVITY: PyTorch is required by torchopt; results are reported within the PyTorch autograd reference, so the JAX control only corroborates, never drives, the decision.\n A3) The \"standard autograd\" control is (i) unrolled PyTorch autograd; control (iii) separates caching from implicit-vs-explicit. SENSITIVITY: if (iii) shows the caching factor accounts for <5% of the difference, conclusion is re-framed as \"explicit-diff advantage is mostly the non-tape structure\".\n A4) Same forward arithmetic across pipelines (bit-identity check). SENSITIVITY: if the treatment's forward differs >machine epsilon, we add a corrected comparison where forward traces are forced identical; otherwise results are confounded.\n A5) Thresholds in the decision rule (10%/10% and 1e-4). SENSITIVITY: provided by the +-5pp / 1e-3-1e-5 sensitivity table, so conclusions are reported as \"robust to +/-5pp\" or \"threshold-sensitive\".\n A6) Execution environment assumption: real run requires torch+torchopt that install cleanly (not true in this workspace). SENSITIVITY: if install fails, the only honest deliverable remains this design (current case).\n A7) CPU-only measurement is acceptable (GPU disabled). SENSITIVITY: absolute times are CPU-specific; all conclusions rely on paired, same-machine ratios, which transfer better than absolute numbers; memory conclusions are architecture-independent.\n\nFIXED REQUIREMENTS (non-negotiable, from the task): same forward solver/iterate sequence across pipelines; >=5 paired repeats; metrics = compute time, memory allocation, numerical error/stability; dimensions = n, T, conditioning (incl. near-singular); controls = standard autograd implementations (primary PyTorch autograd, optional JAX vjp/jacfwd); NO claimed results or executions \u2014 design only.\n\nTHREATS TO VALIDITY (pre-empted): forward arithmetic divergence (A4, bit-identity check); timer contamination (subprocess isolation + warm-ups); allocator noise (deterministic seeding, repeats, paired diffs); reference-error floor dominating kappa effects (choose reference perturb small enough that its own error < treatment/control differences, verified empirically before judging r3); overfitting the decision to one grid point (aggregate over the grid and report per-cell breakdown, not only the average).", "evidence": "Workspace inspection: /workspace contains only task.json; task.json declares 'Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required'. Environment: Python 3.14.4; torchopt, torch, jax all absent (ModuleNotFoundError); no cached wheels; no network (pypi urllib attempt failed; no git/curl/wget). Thus no TorchOpt source could be inspected; the API identity (torchopt.diff.explicit explicit-Jacobian path; FuncOptimizer/accelerated unrolled variant) is taken from the public TorchOpt documentation/paper and embedded as a verifiable assumption with a mandatory API-pinning step. The design fixes identical forward solver and iterate sequences across pipelines, >=5 paired repeats (seeds fixed for exact pairing), metrics of time (forward/backward), peak memory vs T, gradient error vs central finite-difference/analytic reference, and NaN/rel-error on ill-conditioned instances, sweeping n, T, kappa (incl. near-singular) and optional batch. Decision rule (>=10% time, >=10% peak memory, rel error <=1e-4 on well-conditioned set, no stability regression on ill-conditioned set) is set as agreed, with a sensitivity table for the thresholds; no experiment was run and none is claimed.", "assumptions": ["A1 TorchOpt version (target latest stable e.g. 0.7.x) and exact symbol/signature in torchopt.diff.explicit; verifiable via the API-pinning step; harness tolerates symbol differences via a thin wrapper.", "A2 PyTorch is the reference comparison backend; JAX vjp/jacfwd control is optional and conditional on JAX availability, reported separately, not driving the decision.", "A3 The 'standard autograd' control is unrolled PyTorch autograd (memory ~O(T)); a manual torch.autograd.Function implicit-diff control (iii) separates the caching factor from the implicit-vs-explicit factor.", "A4 Forward arithmetic must be bit-identical (max |y*_A - y*_B| ~ machine epsilon) across pipelines; verified before timing, with a forced-identical-trace fallback if violated.", "A5 Decision thresholds (10%/10% speed/memory, 1e-4 gradient rel. error, stability no-regression bound) are agreed; sensitivity reported for +-5pp and 1e-3/1e-5.", "A6 Real execution requires a clean environment where torch+torchopt install (not true in this workspace: Python 3.14, no deps, no network); if install is infeasible the honest deliverable remains this design.", "A7 CPU-only measurement is acceptable; conclusions rely on paired same-machine ratios and architecture-independent memory scaling, not absolute timings."], "uncertainties": ["Exact public symbol name/signature of the explicit-diff entry point in the installed TorchOpt (e.g., torchopt.diff.explicit.explicit_jacobian vs an ExplicitJac class) \u2014 pinned at setup, treated as assumption A1, not confirmed here.", "Whether JAX will actually be available in the execution environment (control ii is conditional).", "Whether the intended interpretation includes the FuncOptimizer (b3) unrolled-optimization flavor; handled as a separate, supplementary variant not merged into the primary decision.", "Whether bit-identical forward arithmetic holds for the explicit path in the real run; protocol includes a verification and a fallback mode.", "Precise runtime budget for the real run (estimate ~10-30 min on CPU for the primary grid) \u2014 to be confirmed with the execution environment."]}", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
